# Clockmaker & Instrument Repair, Business District.
# Mr. Vane: mechanical precision, repairs navigational compasses, escapements, and watches.
NPC: clockmaker
LOCATION: business_district
SCHEDULE: morning=repairer, midday=repairer, evening=repairer, night=closed

TOPIC: default
  GATE: NOT topic_done(clockmaker, default) AND coat = plain
  TAG: clockmaker_plain
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_short_v1
  MR. VANE: "Do not touch the regulator on the wall."
  [The watchmaker works with an eyeglass screwed into his right orbit, tweezers poised over an open brass movement.]
  VOICE: trombone_bureaucratic_medium_v2
  MR. VANE: "The pendulum is calibrated to county mean time. If you have an uncoiled mainspring, set it in the tin dish by the lamp."

TOPIC: default
  GATE: NOT topic_done(clockmaker, default)
  TAG: clockmaker_badge
  VOICE: trombone_haunting_medium_v1
  MR. VANE: "Officer Corwin. I told Captain Odell two years ago that the precinct hall clock gains four minutes a week because of the coal stove beneath it."
  WALTER CORWIN: "I'm not here about the hall clock, Mr. Vane."
  VOICE: trombone_bureaucratic_medium_v2
  MR. VANE: "Then state your gear, Officer. A balance wheel waits for no man."

TOPIC: default
  GATE: visit_count(clockmaker) >= 2 AND NOT (topic_done(clockmaker, the_stopped_watch) AND topic_done(clockmaker, tryworks_instruments))
  TAG: clockmaker_repeat_pendulum
  WEIGHT: 3
  VOICE: trombone_bureaucratic_short_v2
  MR. VANE: "The door draft disturbs the regulator, Officer. Close it firmly."

TOPIC: default
  GATE: visit_count(clockmaker) >= 2 AND NOT (topic_done(clockmaker, the_stopped_watch) AND topic_done(clockmaker, tryworks_instruments))
  TAG: clockmaker_repeat_tweezers
  WEIGHT: 2
  [He remains bent over the jeweler's block, a tiny brass pinion balanced between his tweezers.]
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_short_v1
  MR. VANE: "Speak from the counter, Walter. My breath is timed to this escapement."

TOPIC: default
  GATE: visit_count(clockmaker) >= 2 AND NOT (topic_done(clockmaker, the_stopped_watch) AND topic_done(clockmaker, tryworks_instruments))
  TAG: clockmaker_repeat_escapement
  WEIGHT: 1
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_medium_v2
  MR. VANE: "If a spring hasn't broken, what brings you back?"

TOPIC: default
  GATE: topic_done(clockmaker, the_stopped_watch) AND topic_done(clockmaker, tryworks_instruments)
  TAG: clockmaker_exhausted_oil
  WEIGHT: 3
  VOICE: trombone_dismissive_short_v1
  MR. VANE: "I have said what mechanical inspection permits. The bench is occupied."

TOPIC: default
  GATE: topic_done(clockmaker, the_stopped_watch) AND topic_done(clockmaker, tryworks_instruments)
  TAG: clockmaker_exhausted_eyeglass
  WEIGHT: 2
  [He does not remove the eyeglass from his socket.]
  VOICE: trombone_bureaucratic_short_v1
  MR. VANE: "There are no further dials for you here, Officer."

TOPIC: the_stopped_watch
  GATE: evidence(watch)
  TAG: clockmaker_watch
  LABEL: "Show him the pocket watch stopped at seventeen past three"
  TIME: 8
  WALTER CORWIN: "A gold pocket watch. Found on a body in the rose garden.\nHands stopped at precisely 3:17."
  [Mr. Vane takes the watch with jeweler's tissue, turns it under the magnifier, and taps the balance cock.]
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_medium_v2
  MR. VANE: "English lever movement. London hallmark, circa 1840. The mainspring is fully wound."
  WALTER CORWIN: "Then why did it stop?"
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_medium_v1
  MR. VANE: "The balance staff didn't break, Walter. The hairspring fused."
  WALTER CORWIN: "Fused?"
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_medium_v2
  MR. VANE: "The blued steel coils appear joined at three points. I can show you under the glass. I cannot tell you what joined them."
  WALTER CORWIN: "Does that tell you when it stopped?"
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_medium_v1
  MR. VANE: "It tells me why it cannot run now. The hands are not a certificate of the hour it failed."
  [He pauses, looking up from the loupe with pale, watering eyes.]
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_medium_v2
  MR. VANE: "Though if you want an hour, ask why Judge Wexford, Mr. Corliss, and Dr. Fenn brought me their watches last Thursday."
  WALTER CORWIN: "To be cleaned?"
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_long_v1
  MR. VANE: "To have the second hands removed. Plucked out with tweezers and left in my scrap bin.\nThree men on the same afternoon. They said the jumping of the hand made their parlors too loud."
  NOTEBOOK: clockmaker_watch_1 | "Vane showed fused hairspring in the stopped watch. Revealed Wexford, Corliss, and Fenn had their watches' second hands removed last Thursday: 'the jumping of the hand made their parlors too loud.'"
  EVIDENCE: fused_hairspring_anomaly

TOPIC: tryworks_instruments
  GATE: evidence(watch)
  TAG: clockmaker_maritime
  LABEL: "Ask about old navigational instruments from the fleet"
  TIME: 6
  WALTER CORWIN: "Do you ever see brass work from the 1823 whaling fleet?"
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_medium_v2
  MR. VANE: "The Ophion carried sextants by Troughton of London. Very fine brass, hand-divided silver arcs."
  [He sets down his tweezers and removes the loupe.]
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_medium_v1
  MR. VANE: "A laborer brought me a vernier index two seasons back. Found in the silt off the point.\nHe asked me to polish the verdigris off."
  WALTER CORWIN: "Did you?"
  VOICE: trombone_weary_long_v1
  MR. VANE: "The acid wouldn't touch it. Salt water leaves green crust, Walter. This was dark grey, slick as oil, and smelled of sulphur.\nI gave it back to him. Some metals have had enough done to them."
  NOTEBOOK: clockmaker_instruments_1 | "Watchmaker examined a fleet vernier index from the silt: coated in dark, acid-resistant grey patina that smelled of sulphur."
  EVIDENCE: tryworks_metal_patina
